WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.100
STEPHANIE WONG: Cloud
Functions gives developers

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.900
quite an ideal experience.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.390
You write code, you give
Cloud Functions your code,

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.700
and Cloud Functions takes
care of everything else

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.310
to run it in a fully managed
production environment,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.890
ready to run thousands of
invocations of your code

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.090
at any given time.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.550
One of the defining traits
of serverless platforms,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.620
like Cloud Functions,
is that you get

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.210
to focus on writing your code.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.870
The platform handles the rest.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.340
"Serverless" means that these
operations get abstracted away

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.930
from the developer experience.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.930
Those abstractions can
also be constraints.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.750
Your code will only go
as far as those use cases

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.970
that the underlying
platform can support.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.910
This is why we are introducing
Cloud Functions 2nd generation,

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.850
a new version of
Cloud Functions built

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.650
to bring new functionality
to the platform.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.820
This means Cloud Functions can
now serve even more developer

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.640
use cases, all while
maintaining the same developer

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.410
contract and simplicity.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.710
Are you concerned about running
a bit more intensive code

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.150
in Cloud Functions?

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.670
We've bumped up the
amount of resources

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.430
an instance of your
code can use to four

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.550
CPUs and 16 gigabytes of RAM.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.728
Do you spend extra time
working through how

00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:09.270
to roll out new
versions of your code

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.630
to only serve a portion of
requests or invocations?

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.690
Cloud Functions 2nd gen makes
this process declarative.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.520
You can now just
tell Cloud Functions

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.460
how traffic should be split
between different versions

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.720
of your code.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.350
All of this is possible because
Cloud Functions 2nd generation

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.870
re-platformed its
underlying infrastructure

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.590
to use Google Cloud's
Container Service, Cloud Run.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.940
But don't worry.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.950
Despite this change,
the experience

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.750
remains the same for developers.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.390
Write code, and deploy
to Cloud Functions.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.360
Finally, 2nd gen will
also greatly expand

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.390
the sources that can trigger
your code's execution.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.640
Over 90-plus event
sources are now

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.280
natively supported through
Eventarc, new sources

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:50.863
like BigQuery.

00:01:50.863 --> 00:01:52.363
For many of you,
this means that you

00:01:52.363 --> 00:01:54.180
won't have to wire
together custom code

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.710
to trigger their
code from sources

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.100
that previously were not
supported in Cloud Functions.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.680
Whether or not you're
a Cloud Functions pro,

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.600
we encourage you to
get started by looking

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.270
at our documentation, our
codelab that walks you

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.432
through these new features,
or even directly visiting

00:02:09.432 --> 00:02:11.640
the Cloud Console, which
has been updated to help you

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.190
seamlessly onboard into
deploying your code to Cloud

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.060
Functions.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.210
This is Stephanie Wong.

